      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "There is no single \"definitive\" answer because the Social Contract theory itself is a family of ideas that contest this exact question in opposite ways. Hobbesian theorists like to see state power (and by extension surveillance) as a necessary evolution to ensure peace at the cost of liberty, while Locke and Rousseau would argue it violates the core bargain of natural rights unless strictly limited. Since you cannot take a factual position on a moral philosophy debate without choosing a specific school of thought, the only fact you can write down is that the \"inherent\" nature depends entirely on which version of the contract your professor is using as the framework.",
